This plugin vendors two skills from Lauren Tan's "pstack" plugin, shipped in Cursor's
official plugin repo:

    https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack

    pstack/skills/unslop/SKILL.md            -> skills/unslop/SKILL.md
    pstack/skills/technical-writing/SKILL.md -> skills/technical-writing/SKILL.md

Upstream commit:

    f5bdd6826fd0a0d9cbc4347134c3a74a200b9d9d

SKILL.md is the whole of each skill upstream: no references/, no scripts, no
agents/openai.yaml, nothing else to carry. The rest of pstack (agents, automations,
docs) is a Cursor-oriented plugin and is deliberately not vendored here.

The two are a pair, which is why they share one plugin and one pin. unslop is the
slop-pattern catalog with stable rule ids; technical-writing is the four-layer
standard that cites it and carries the voice guidance that used to live in unslop.
Splitting them across two plugins would mean two pins against one upstream repo, and
the first symptom of drift would be technical-writing citing a rule the vendored
unslop no longer has.

The upstream repo root carries no licence file. `pstack/LICENSE` is MIT and covers
both files; it is reproduced below.

LOCAL MODIFICATIONS: unslop
---------------------------

None. skills/unslop/SKILL.md is byte-for-byte upstream.

That description promises more than the mechanism delivers. A skill description is an
advertisement the model must still act on each turn, and it reliably goes unacted on
for prose-shaped turns, which is exactly where unslop matters. Upstream now also sets
`disable-model-invocation: true`, so the model cannot reach the skill on its own at
all. Delivery is therefore a SessionStart hook (`hooks/scripts/inject-unslop.mjs`)
that reads this file at runtime, strips the frontmatter, and injects the body inside
a plugin-local scoping wrapper: full ruleset for written deliverables; jargon and
plain-speech rules (26-31 and 33) for technical explanations and design discussion in
chat; the active output style wins mechanical style conflicts (rule 13's em-dash ban,
rule 26's "harness"/"surface") in conversation, unslop wins them in deliverables.

Rule 32 (mannered prose) is deliberately NOT in the chat range. It bans aphorisms,
rhetorical fragments and figurative verbs, which is the conversational register the
active output style exists to produce; the parts of it that matter for an explanation
are already banned by that style. Rule 33 (over-compression) is in the chat range,
because dropped articles and arrow-speak hurt an explanation the same way in either
place. Both apply in full to deliverables.

The rule numbers have gaps. Upstream treats them as stable ids that other skills cite
and leaves a hole rather than renumbering when a rule is retired; rules 1, 2, 4, 6 and
21 were general-prose rules retired upstream. Do not renumber to close a gap. Scoping changes belong
in the wrapper; a rule change would go through a patch under `patches/`, following
the `plugins/grill` pattern - none exists today. The vendored file itself stays
byte-for-byte upstream.

LOCAL MODIFICATIONS: technical-writing
--------------------------------------

skills/technical-writing/SKILL.md is NOT verbatim. Two patches apply on top of
upstream, in order:

    patches/0001-narrow-the-invocation-gate.patch
    patches/0002-repo-specifics.patch

0001 removes `disable-model-invocation: true` and narrows the description that
replaces it. Upstream gates the skill behind a typed `/technical-writing`, which
means it covers nothing on the turns where it would help most. We want the model to
reach for it, so the gate has to come off and the trigger has to earn its keep
instead. Upstream's trigger list ends in "PR descriptions, or commit messages", and a
conventional commit subject does not need eleven kilobytes of Diátaxis. The
replacement fires on prose a reader comes back to and refuses two triggers: a bare
commit subject, and chat. Chat matters because the Global English layer bans idioms,
Latin, and metaphor, which is most of what the active output style exists to produce.
That style governs conversation, and this skill governs documents.

Those are rules about when to invoke the skill, not about which words it then covers,
and the description now says so. An earlier wording said "not for commit subjects"
flatly, which contradicted the body's "PR descriptions and commit messages are writing
too. Every layer except Diátaxis applies to them", since a commit message contains its
subject. CodeRabbit caught it on the PR that vendored this file. The body stays
upstream-verbatim and the frontmatter carries the distinction.

0002 fixes three lines that describe pstack's repo rather than this one. "Indent code
snippets with tabs" is a Cursor convention and this repo is spaces, so the line now
says to match the file. "Apply the unslop skill" cannot be done here, or upstream: the
hook has already put the rules in context and the skill sets
`disable-model-invocation: true`, so the line now says so rather than sending a model
after a skill it cannot call. The jargon paragraph's "Don't edit that skill" gains the
destination a change actually goes to, which is patches/ in this directory.

One file is added rather than patched:

    skills/technical-writing/agents/openai.yaml

Upstream ships no openai.yaml for any pstack skill. Codex decides whether a model may
reach a skill on its own from `policy.allow_implicit_invocation`, so the Codex half of
0001's decision lives in this file. A re-sync copies SKILL.md only and leaves this file
alone. skills/unslop/ needs no equivalent, because its rules arrive through the hook
on both hosts.

Two upstream lines are deliberately NOT patched. "Do not paste swarm logs, SHA lists,
or metric tables" sits near the charter's "one-line-per-commit changelog" for a PR
body, and the two do not actually conflict: a list of commit subjects is not a list of
SHAs. "Use your product's copy guidelines" for UI strings is left pointing nowhere on
purpose, because the skill that would answer it here comes from a marketplace this
repo does not control.

RE-SYNCING
----------

    1. Copy pstack/skills/unslop/SKILL.md over skills/unslop/SKILL.md
    2. Copy pstack/skills/technical-writing/SKILL.md over
       skills/technical-writing/SKILL.md
    3. git apply patches/0001-narrow-the-invocation-gate.patch
    4. git apply patches/0002-repo-specifics.patch
    5. Update the commit SHA above; regenerate any patch that stops applying
    6. Re-measure the hook payloads, which the unslop body feeds:
       echo '{"agent_type":"claude"}' | node hooks/scripts/inject-unslop.mjs subagent claude | wc -c
       Claude Code swaps a hook stdout over 10,000 characters for a 2KB preview.

The patches are ordinary `git diff` output with `a/plugins/unslop/skills/...` paths,
so apply them from the repo root. To regenerate one: commit the state it applies to,
redo the change on top of that, and `git diff` the two commits. Verify the chain by
applying it to a fresh upstream copy in a scratch directory and diffing the result
against skills/. They must be byte-identical.

